{"parentUuid":"4703d180-3d16-405a-a035-1823e52b1da9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a2dc46b9af8a17e14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KZp3UK74FmQk3xCXpYAjMd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Reformulation Linearization Technique RLT 2D bin packing strip packing 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Robust Optimization for the Two-Dimensional Strip-Packing Problem with Variable-Sized Bins\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/11/23/4781\"},{\"title\":\"Reformulation–Linearization Techniques for Discrete Optimization Problems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-1-4419-7997-1_45\"},{\"title\":\"Tight Relaxations for Nonconvex Optimization Problems Using the Reformulation-Linearization/Convexification Technique (RLT) | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4757-5362-2_1\"},{\"title\":\"Reformulation–Linearization Techniques for Mixed-Discrete Optimization Problems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/rwe/10.1007/978-1-4614-6624-6_45-1\"},{\"title\":\"RLT-POS: Reformulation-Linearization Technique-based optimization software for solving polynomial programming problems | Mathematical Programming Computation | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-016-0099-5\"},{\"title\":\"Multidimensional Bin Packing and Other Related Problems: A Survey ∗\",\"url\":\"https://tetali.math.gatech.edu/PUBLIS/CKPT.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Robust mixed-integer linear programming models for the irregular strip packing problem | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/297889945_Robust_mixed-integer_linear_programming_models_for_the_irregular_strip_packing_problem\"},{\"title\":\"A new reformulation-linearization technique for bilinear programming problems | Journal of Global Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00122429\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing two dimensional irregular pattern packing using a modified genetic algorithm and image processing - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0952197625010401\"},{\"title\":\"A Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) for semi-infinite and convex programs under mixed 0-1 and general discrete restrictions - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X08002011\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about the Reformulation Linearization Technique (RLT) and its applications to 2D bin packing and strip packing:\n\n## Overview of RLT\n\nThe Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) is used for solving mixed-integer 0-1 and general mixed-discrete optimization problems. RLT can be used for directly lifting mixed-integer programs (MIPs) into higher-dimensional representations as well as for deriving strong valid inequalities in the space of the original variables.\n\n## Applications to Packing Problems\n\nThe search results show several applications of RLT-related techniques to packing problems:\n\n- Robust optimization approaches have been developed to address the multi-strip 2D strip packing problem with demand uncertainty, employing a carefully constructed box uncertainty set to manage the complexities.\n\n- The 2D strip packing problem (2D-SPP) can be reformulated as a 1D-contiguous bin-packing problem (1D-CBPP) through contiguous one-dimensional relaxation.\n\n- RLT-POS is an open-source optimization software for solving polynomial programming problems, featuring algorithms including constraint filtering techniques, reduced RLT representations, and semidefinite cuts.\n\nThe search results do not contain specific 2024-2025 publications directly combining RLT with 2D bin packing or strip packing, though recent work on irregular packing problems and optimization of cutting and packing continues to be active research areas.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"2598da2d-447b-45b1-9723-d6c54cea98f3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:05.374Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4703d180-3d16-405a-a035-1823e52b1da9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"2362f2e9-b1f1-4344-9a81-c08cf2e267d0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a2dc46b9af8a17e14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MEFyC47SntBbZmRRVi44NB","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Sherali-Adams RLT hierarchy bilinear binary 0-1 quadratic programming 2025 implementation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Mixed-integer bilinear programming problems | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Mixed-integer-bilinear-programming-problems-Adams-Sherali/2f931ef26f0c94a0472b535652b6e98345887057\"},{\"title\":\"A Hierarchy of Relaxations Leading to the Convex Hull Representation for General Discrete Optimization Problems | Annals of Operations Research | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10479-005-3966-4\"},{\"title\":\"A hierarchy of relaxations and convex hull characterizations for mixed-integer zero—one programming problems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0166218X9200190W\"},{\"title\":\"RLT insights into lift-and-project closures | Optimization Letters | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11590-014-0763-5\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] A Hierarchy of Relaxations and Convex Hull Characterizations for Mixed-integer Zero-one Programming Problems | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/A-Hierarchy-of-Relaxations-and-Convex-Hull-for-Sherali-Adams/b7cd2561409b7ce7cb6a577937d3a45fa3be95a7\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry in RLT-type relaxations for the quadratic assignment and standard quadratic optimization problems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0377221713008308\"},{\"title\":\"Mixed-integer bilinear programming problems | Mathematical Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01581249\"},{\"title\":\"Extending SDP Integrality Gaps to Sherali-Adams with Applications to Quadratic Programming and MaxCutGain | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-13036-6_23\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) A Comparison of the Sherali-Adams, Lovász-Schrijver and Lasserre Relaxations for 0-1 Programming\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2521821_A_Comparison_of_the_Sherali-Adams_Lovasz-Schrijver_and_Lasserre_Relaxations_for_0-1_Programming\"},{\"title\":\"Sherali-Adams Relaxations of the Matching Polytope Claire Mathieu∗\",\"url\":\"https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sinclair/sherali.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding the Sherali-Adams RLT hierarchy and its application to bilinear binary 0-1 quadratic programming:\n\n## Overview of Sherali-Adams RLT Hierarchy\n\nThe reformulation-linearization technique (RLT) generates a hierarchy of linear programming relaxations that spans the spectrum from the continuous relaxation to the convex hull representation for linear mixed-integer programs where variables are restricted to discrete values.\n\n## Application to Bilinear and Quadratic Programming\n\nA reformulation-linearization technique (RLT) has been developed to generate a hierarchy of linear programming relaxations that spans from the continuous relaxation to the convex hull representation for mixed-integer bilinear programming problems. These problems are identical to the well known bilinear programming problems with the exception that one set of variables is restricted to be binary valued, and they arise in various production, location—allocation, and distribution application contexts.\n\n## Methodology\n\nThe technique first converts the problem into a nonlinear, polynomial mixed-integer zero—one problem by multiplying the constraints with suitable d-degree polynomial factors involving the binary variables, and subsequently linearizes the resulting problem through appropriate variable transformations. As d varies from zero to n, a hierarchy of relaxations is obtained spanning from the ordinary linear programming relaxation to the convex hull of feasible solutions.\n\n## Comparison with Other Hierarchies\n\nSherali and Adams, Lovász and Schrijver, and Lasserre have proposed lift and project methods for constructing hierarchies of successive linear or semidefinite relaxations of 0-1 polytopes converging in n steps. The Lasserre construction provides the tightest relaxations.\n\nThe search results contain foundational theoretical work but do not appear to include specific 2025 implementation details.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e22c5466-1c82-4102-9fc5-5bf976815cdf","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:07.395Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2362f2e9-b1f1-4344-9a81-c08cf2e267d0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
