{"parentUuid":"c79e3f28-bacb-4699-9177-e32f7190ae2c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a2dc46b9af8a17e14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ANA3iihHVx5nG8w5oVNDHP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"RLT-POS RLT-POPCO Sherali Adams software polynomial programming open source 2024\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"RLT-POS: Reformulation-Linearization Technique-based optimization software for solving polynomial programming problems | Mathematical Programming Computation | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12532-016-0099-5\"},{\"title\":\"RLT-POS: Reformulation-Linearization Technique-based optimization software for solving polynomial programming problems | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/293043079_RLT-POS_Reformulation-Linearization_Technique-based_optimization_software_for_solving_polynomial_programming_problems\"},{\"title\":\"RLT-POS: Reformulation-Linearization Technique-based Optimization Software for Solving Polynomial Programming Problems | Argonne Leadership Computing Facility\",\"url\":\"https://alcf.anl.gov/events/rlt-pos-reformulation-linearization-technique-based-optimization-software-solving-polynomial\"},{\"title\":\"Reduced RLT representations for nonconvex polynomial programming problems | Journal of Global Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10898-011-9757-3\"},{\"title\":\"Computational advances in polynomial optimization: RAPOSa, a freely available global solver | Journal of Global Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10898-022-01229-w\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting planes for RLT relaxations of mixed 0–1 polynomial programs | Mathematical Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-015-0863-8\"},{\"title\":\"Polynomial Optimization: Tightening RLT-Based Branch-and-Bound Schemes with Conic Constraints | Journal of Optimization Theory and Applications\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1007/s10957-024-02558-4\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting Planes for RLT Relaxations of Mixed 0-1 Polynomial Programs\",\"url\":\"https://api.newton.ac.uk/website/v0/events/preprints/NI14004\"},{\"title\":\"A Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) for semi-infinite and convex programs under mixed 0-1 and general discrete restrictions | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/222083578_A_Reformulation-Linearization_Technique_RLT_for_semi-infinite_and_convex_programs_under_mixed_0-1_and_general_discrete_restrictions\"},{\"title\":\"Symmetry in RLT cuts for the quadratic assignment and standard quadratic optimization problems – Optimization Online\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/2012/01/3303/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found:\n\n## RLT-POS: Reformulation-Linearization Technique-based Optimization Software\n\nRLT-POS is a Reformulation-Linearization Technique-based open-source optimization software for solving polynomial programming problems. The software was published in 2016 in the Mathematical Programming Computation journal.\n\n## Key Information\n\nThe software is based on the work of Hanif D. Sherali and Warren P. Adams, who developed the Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) methodology. The reformulation–linearization technique, due to Sherali and Adams, can be used to construct hierarchies of linear programming relaxations of mixed 0–1 polynomial programs.\n\n## Related Software\n\nIt's worth noting that there is also another related tool called RAPOSa: RAPOSa (Reformulation Algorithm for Polynomial Optimization—Santiago) is a global optimization solver specifically designed for polynomial programming problems with box-constrained variables, and it is based on the Reformulation-Linearization Technique and has been implemented in C++.\n\nThe search results did not contain specific information about \"RLT-POPCO\" or any 2024 updates to these tools, so I cannot provide details about those aspects based on the available search results.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"1d058ce2-3db8-4b05-858a-692a5b3ea86a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:19.074Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c79e3f28-bacb-4699-9177-e32f7190ae2c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b9d05980-5124-4ff9-83f4-fd456ee0b418","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a2dc46b9af8a17e14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MhVR4Pyqpj1tFuWV6W9ALC","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"RLT facility location placement valid inequalities cutting planes 2024 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Solution of stochastic facility location problems with combinatorially many decision-dependent distributions | Journal of Combinatorial Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10878-025-01371-7\"},{\"title\":\"Multicommodity Multifacility Network Design | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-64018-7_5\"},{\"title\":\"Enhancing RLT relaxations via a new class of semidefinite cuts | Journal of Global Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1023/A:1013819515732\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting Planes for RLT Relaxations of Mixed 0-1 ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.newton.ac.uk/files/preprints/ni14004_0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Capacitated facility location: Separation algorithms and computational experience | Mathematical Programming\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01581103\"},{\"title\":\"Simultaneous convexification for the planar obnoxious facility location problem | Journal of Global Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10898-025-01464-x\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting Planes for RLT Relaxations of Mixed 0-1 Polynomial Programs\",\"url\":\"https://api.newton.ac.uk/website/v0/events/preprints/NI14004\"},{\"title\":\"Cover-based inequalities for the single-source capacitated facility location problem with customer preferences – Optimization Online\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/2024/08/cover-based-inequalities-for-the-single-source-capacitated-facility-location-problem-with-customer-preferences/\"},{\"title\":\"Achieving Consistency with Cutting Planes - John Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/MIPconsistency2021.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Facility location problem - Cornell University Computational Optimization Open Textbook - Optimization Wiki\",\"url\":\"https://optimization.cbe.cornell.edu/index.php?title=Facility_location_problem\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query on RLT facility location placement valid inequalities and cutting planes for 2024-2025. Here's what I found:\n\n## Recent Research Findings\n\n**RLT and Cutting Planes:**\n\nRecent work proposes mechanisms to tighten Reformulation-Linearization Technique (RLT) based relaxations for solving nonconvex programming problems by importing concepts from semidefinite programming, leading to new classes of semidefinite cutting planes. The approach replaces the usual nonnegativity restrictions on the matrix of RLT product variables with a suitable positive semidefinite constraint, which is re-written to develop a semi-infinite linear programming representation that can be implemented using traditional optimization software.\n\n**Facility Location with Cutting Planes:**\n\nValid inequalities used as cutting planes in branch-and-bound algorithms have been instrumental in solving large-scale network design instances. Research on the polyhedral approach to solving the capacitated facility location problem considers valid inequalities including knapsack cover, flow cover, effective capacity, single depot, and combinatorial inequalities.\n\n**Recent 2024-2025 Applications:**\n\nRecent work on two-stage facility location problems with decision-dependent uncertainty derives distribution-specific cutting planes for which validity is shown, and demonstrates that these cuts ensure finite convergence of exact decomposition algorithms.\n\nAnother 2025 study addresses planar obnoxious facility location problems by characterizing the simultaneous convex hull of the intersection of reverse convex reduced domains, deriving tighter variable bounds and optimality-based cutting planes.\n\nThe search results show active research in combining RLT relaxations with sophisticated cutting plane techniques for facility location problems, particularly focusing on improving solution quality and computational efficiency.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"e4cc69f2-785e-4a07-9063-a1461c94f023","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:19.584Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b9d05980-5124-4ff9-83f4-fd456ee0b418","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"2bbfad70-e570-4272-b01f-baeb8f19c3cd","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a2dc46b9af8a17e14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gxvdpo5HMxJKXx5bDCj4UF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT cutting planes user cuts external cut generator OR-tools integration\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Cut generators in CPModelProto · Issue #912 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/912\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting Planes in Constraint Programming: An Hybrid Approach | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-45349-0_15\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting Plane - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/cutting-plane\"},{\"title\":\"Computational Integer Programming Lecture 11: Cutting Plane Methods\",\"url\":\"https://coral.ise.lehigh.edu/~ted/files/computational-mip/lectures/Lecture11.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting Plane Algorithm - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/computer-science/cutting-plane-algorithm\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting-plane method - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cutting-plane_method\"},{\"title\":\"Developing Customized Branch-&-Cut algorithms — Python-MIP documentation\",\"url\":\"https://python-mip.readthedocs.io/en/latest/custom.html\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting Planes\",\"url\":\"https://www.hexaly.com/algorithms/cutting-planes\"},{\"title\":\"Cutting planes in integer and mixed integer programming - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0166218X01003481\"},{\"title\":\"[2311.03350] Differentiable Cutting-plane Layers for Mixed-integer Linear Optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.03350\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding CP-SAT cutting planes, user cuts, and external cut generators in OR-tools:\n\n## OR-tools CP-SAT and Cutting Planes\n\nAccording to a GitHub issue on the OR-tools repository, users have attempted to implement custom cutting planes over a SAT model using CutGenerator callbacks, but were unable to find an interface on the CPModelProto that allows for adding a cut-callback.\n\nThis limitation appears to be architectural—passing a pointer to a callback over the proto model is considered impractical, and support for cut generators may not be available at the CPModel level in OR-tools.\n\n## General Context on Cutting Planes\n\nFor broader context, the cutting-plane method is a variety of optimization methods that iteratively refine a feasible set or objective function by means of linear inequalities, termed cuts. Cutting planes are a foundational element of modern MILP solvers that significantly enhance the effectiveness of Branch-and-Bound by tightening relaxations early in the search.\n\nThe search results suggest that while cutting plane generation is well-established in mathematical optimization, the integration of external/user-defined cut generators specifically into OR-tools' CP-SAT solver presents architectural challenges, particularly at the higher-level CPModel API level.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"a6a42136-7ce0-4139-8ca2-1a39cdceddf4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:25:20.393Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2bbfad70-e570-4272-b01f-baeb8f19c3cd","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
