{"parentUuid":"241c8f8b-c853-4c43-944b-29287d2f1934","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a2dc46b9af8a17e14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Request failed with status code 303","is_error":true,"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H3yVdrym2p8qTkKFEQ9Ko2"}]},"uuid":"57c9735c-4552-407f-b4df-d23d52b13544","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:04.696Z","toolUseResult":"Error: Request failed with status code 303","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"241c8f8b-c853-4c43-944b-29287d2f1934","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"b20b6f3e-f153-41ae-ab4a-4ec2d1aed8e9","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"11f7002f-1250-416f-bd07-34f6030c1de7","agentId":"a2dc46b9af8a17e14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TpPQ2UZGZ8MgrNDj4sUWU8","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"orthogonal packing\" non-overlap bilinear xi*yj position assignment formulation tight LP relaxation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A new search procedure for the two-dimensional orthogonal ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.dil.univ-mrs.fr/~gcolas/packing/jmma-20022015.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"LP bounds in various constraint programming approaches for orthogonal packing - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0305054811003650\"},{\"title\":\"Bidimensional Packing by Bilinear Programming | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/221316836_Bidimensional_Packing_by_Bilinear_Programming\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Orthogonal Packings in Two Dimensions\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220617342_Orthogonal_Packings_in_Two_Dimensions\"},{\"title\":\"An Exact Algorithm for Higher-Dimensional Orthogonal Packing | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/1959259_An_Exact_Algorithm_for_Higher-Dimensional_Orthogonal_Packing\"},{\"title\":\"The Bottom-Left Algorithm for the Strip Packing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2402.16572\"},{\"title\":\"A General Framework for Bounds for Higher-Dimensional ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/cs/0402044\"},{\"title\":\"A new exact method for the two-dimensional orthogonal packing problem | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/223389474_A_new_exact_method_for_the_two-dimensional_orthogonal_packing_problem\"},{\"title\":\"A new exact method for the two-dimensional orthogonal packing problem - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0377221706002992\"},{\"title\":\"Bidimensional packing by bilinear programming | Mathematical Programming | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10107-007-0184-7\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about orthogonal packing with connections to bilinear programming and position assignment formulations. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Orthogonal Packing and Bilinear Programming\n\nGeometric problems in which rectangles have to be packed into squares turn out to be very hard in practice, and ILP formulations in which variables specify the coordinates in the packing perform very poorly.\n\n## Position Assignment Formulations\n\nThe basic question \"Does a given set of rectangles fit into a square?\" is the bottleneck of all the approaches known. Given a set of rectangles and the associated convex hull of rectangle subsets that fit into a square, a wide class of valid inequalities can be derived from a complete description of the two knapsack polytopes associated with the widths and the heights of the rectangles, respectively.\n\n## Bilinear Programming Approach\n\nThe associated separation problem can be solved as a bilinear program. This relates to the xi*yj terms you mentioned, which typically appear in bilinear formulations where xi represents position variables in one dimension and yj represents position variables in another.\n\n## Non-overlapping Constraints\n\nA feasible packing layout requires all items to be allocated orthogonally and non-overlapped within the container.\n\nThe search results indicate that LP-based pruning rules have been embedded into constraint propagation processes for 2D strip packing and orthogonal packing problems.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d9792ddb-0963-4de8-bd83-234dccc4e969","timestamp":"2026-05-07T22:26:10.198Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b20b6f3e-f153-41ae-ab4a-4ec2d1aed8e9","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd\\.upstream_clones","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
